1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,060
Which surface appears at a screen location,

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:07,420
and why does it have that color and
brightness?

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:10,200
We begin with occlusion in real games and
distinguish

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,320
methods for selecting visible surfaces.

5
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:16,020
Then we calculate light quantities and units
and examine

6
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:19,960
how material and view direction affect
reflection.

7
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:23,340
Finally we connect them with the rendering

8
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:26,540
equation and solve a simple brightness
problem.

9
00:00:27,183 --> 00:00:31,763
Follow a character among rocks and trees in
Big Walk.

10
00:00:32,003 --> 00:00:36,253
Observe where the character overlaps
foreground rock.

11
00:00:37,843 --> 00:00:42,183
Moving the camera makes the body disappear
behind rock and reappear.

12
00:00:42,503 --> 00:00:47,032
Several surfaces compete for a screen
location.

13
00:00:48,782 --> 00:00:53,742
For opaque surfaces, the first surface along
that camera

14
00:00:55,431 --> 00:00:57,731
direction is visible; the hidden object still
exists.

15
00:00:57,731 --> 00:01:04,881
Selecting that surface and determining its
illumination are separate questions.

16
00:01:06,751 --> 00:01:12,400
Adding light does not automatically make an
opaque foreground rock transparent.

17
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:15,600
First select the surface to evaluate.

18
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:19,270
Our independent diagram compares two basic
approaches

19
00:01:19,270 --> 00:01:23,530
without inferring this game implementation.

20
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:27,270
Rendering outputs colors at image samples. A
framebuffer stores them,

21
00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:32,290
possibly followed by display postprocessing.

22
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:35,990
Ray-based visibility constructs a camera ray
for a screen

23
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:40,490
sample and finds the nearest valid scene
intersection.

24
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:45,290
An intersection supplies position,

25
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:48,130
normal and material for shading toward the
camera; a miss uses the background.

26
00:01:48,143 --> 00:01:50,710
Rasterization starts with projected triangles,

27
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:55,150
determines sample coverage and compares
depths.

28
00:01:55,490 --> 00:01:59,490
The outer traversal differs: screen samples or
scene primitives.

29
00:01:59,830 --> 00:02:02,630
Both address visibility and shading.

30
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:08,210
A pixel need not use exactly one ray. Multiple
samples help reconstruction,

31
00:02:08,430 --> 00:02:11,730
and rays alone do not guarantee accurate
illumination.

32
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:16,430
Red and blue triangles cover one sample.

33
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:21,790
In our toy convention smaller is nearer, with
depths eight and three.

34
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:26,530
Initialize depth to infinity. Red eight passes
first;

35
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:31,270
blue three then replaces its color and depth.

36
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:38,370
Reverse the order: blue three is stored first,
and the farther red eight is rejected.

37
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:42,130
Both orders produce blue at depth three

38
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:47,530
under the defined opaque depth rules.

39
00:02:48,130 --> 00:02:53,390
These are explanatory values. Actual depth
buffers usually

40
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:56,110
store transformed projected depth rather than
metric distance.

41
00:02:56,470 --> 00:02:59,170
Reversed depth uses the opposite order.
Initialize and compare consistently,

42
00:02:59,630 --> 00:03:04,890
and define equal-depth behavior separately.

43
00:03:05,517 --> 00:03:08,737
Observe characters through circular openings
in the yellow structure,

44
00:03:09,037 --> 00:03:16,713
distinguishing wall and opening.

45
00:03:21,848 --> 00:03:25,688
No wall surface blocks the opening.

46
00:03:30,844 --> 00:03:34,144
This differs from making one opaque wall color
faint.

47
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:42,580
Next, examine the transparent tank near the
player

48
00:03:44,353 --> 00:03:47,273
hands: its surface and the red interior are
both visible.

49
00:03:48,945 --> 00:03:52,345
Transparency requires more than selecting one
front surface:

50
00:03:54,138 --> 00:03:58,138
transmitted background light and sometimes
refraction matter.

51
00:03:58,138 --> 00:04:02,531
These visible differences motivate the
explanation

52
00:04:04,423 --> 00:04:08,663
but do not identify the game transparency
technique.

53
00:04:09,603 --> 00:04:14,583
Forward shading evaluates material and
illumination while processing primitives.

54
00:04:14,883 --> 00:04:19,543
Optimization can change actual depth-test and
shading order.

55
00:04:19,607 --> 00:04:22,983
Deferred shading first stores visible-surface
information

56
00:04:23,043 --> 00:04:27,443
in a G-buffer: depth or position, normal and
material.

57
00:04:27,963 --> 00:04:32,063
A later pass reads that information and
evaluates light contributions,

58
00:04:32,503 --> 00:04:36,723
separating triangle-surface processing from
lighting calculation.

59
00:04:36,723 --> 00:04:40,783
Many lights can benefit from reusing surface
information,

60
00:04:41,183 --> 00:04:45,903
at the cost of buffer memory, bandwidth and
transparency handling.

61
00:04:46,343 --> 00:04:50,683
Modern tiled or clustered forward methods need
not redraw every

62
00:04:50,883 --> 00:04:56,023
triangle for each light. Neither organization
always wins.

63
00:04:56,303 --> 00:04:58,103
This reorganizes computation rather than
changing visibility or reflection laws;

64
00:04:58,403 --> 00:05:01,243
complex transmission and scattering within
space,

65
00:05:01,423 --> 00:05:06,343
such as fog, require additional handling.

66
00:05:07,017 --> 00:05:11,857
A red surface can change highlights as light
and camera positions change;

67
00:05:12,177 --> 00:05:15,997
one color cannot describe all directions.

68
00:05:16,217 --> 00:05:23,037
A BRDF describes directional reflection at one
surface location.

69
00:05:23,437 --> 00:05:29,597
It depends on location, incident and outgoing
directions

70
00:05:29,997 --> 00:05:33,557
and wavelength or a chosen color
representation.

71
00:05:33,557 --> 00:05:38,117
We define the incident-direction vector from
the

72
00:05:38,717 --> 00:05:42,457
surface toward the source, opposite actual
light travel.

73
00:05:42,917 --> 00:05:47,697
The outgoing vector points toward the viewer
and the normal points outward;

74
00:05:48,297 --> 00:05:51,197
these conventions determine cosine signs.

75
00:05:51,577 --> 00:05:55,237
Distinguish broad reflection from a narrow
directional

76
00:05:55,577 --> 00:05:59,397
lobe that a changing view can intersect or
miss.

77
00:06:00,183 --> 00:06:09,895
In the Subnautica2 base, compare a white wall
and dark storage surfaces as the camera turns.

78
00:06:12,167 --> 00:06:16,307
Approaching the bright base from dark water
changes the view.

79
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:24,000
Both material response and illumination affect
pixels.

80
00:06:28,133 --> 00:06:32,833
The bright patterns change along walls and
ceiling;

81
00:06:33,033 --> 00:06:38,164
green construction previews are visible game
tool overlays.

82
00:06:38,404 --> 00:06:44,876
Finished pixels alone do not uniquely identify
a BRDF or light value.

83
00:06:44,896 --> 00:06:49,267
Our independent example explicitly defines
conditions to

84
00:06:49,267 --> 00:06:52,247
distinguish a reflection distribution from
energy conservation.

85
00:06:52,867 --> 00:06:56,927
A BRDF is not a scalar reflection probability
between zero and one;

86
00:06:57,127 --> 00:07:00,547
its directional density has
inverse-solid-angle units.

87
00:07:00,827 --> 00:07:05,127
A narrow lobe can exceed one

88
00:07:05,407 --> 00:07:08,487
locally without creating energy.

89
00:07:08,847 --> 00:07:14,567
Energy conservation tests the cosine-weighted
integral over outgoing

90
00:07:14,867 --> 00:07:18,507
directions: passive reflection cannot exceed
incident energy.

91
00:07:18,667 --> 00:07:20,487
The distribution must be nonnegative;

92
00:07:20,487 --> 00:07:25,847
reciprocal ordinary materials have the same
response after exchanging directions.

93
00:07:26,007 --> 00:07:30,287
Emission is a separate contribution.

94
00:07:30,467 --> 00:07:34,607
More complex phenomena such as fluorescence
require broader models.

95
00:07:34,747 --> 00:07:38,687
Historical unnormalized Blinn–Phong formulas
can violate conservation;

96
00:07:38,807 --> 00:07:42,147
a familiar highlight model is not the whole
physical law.

97
00:07:42,833 --> 00:07:49,333
Reflection returns light to the same side of
the surface; a BRDF describes that case.

98
00:07:49,613 --> 00:07:53,093
A BTDF describes transmission.

99
00:07:53,333 --> 00:07:58,413
A BSDF combines reflection and transmission at
the same location.

100
00:07:59,093 --> 00:08:03,553
Subsurface scattering can move light between
different entry and exit positions;

101
00:08:03,773 --> 00:08:07,653
a BSSRDF distinguishes both locations.

102
00:08:07,833 --> 00:08:12,773
Adding directions and adding a second position
are different extensions;

103
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:16,373
do not conflate repeated source abbreviations.

104
00:08:16,713 --> 00:08:19,333
Fog and underwater scattering require volume
scattering,

105
00:08:19,753 --> 00:08:23,673
absorption and directional distributions
beyond surface reflection.

106
00:08:23,973 --> 00:08:28,313
Our calculations concern an opaque surface
with reflection and emission,

107
00:08:28,633 --> 00:08:32,493
rather than all underwater effects in one
surface equation.

108
00:08:33,117 --> 00:08:37,617
Move from bright outdoors into a red structure
in Big Walk,

109
00:08:40,731 --> 00:08:43,551
observing red surfaces and shaded regions.

110
00:08:46,765 --> 00:08:50,045
The next shot shows blue instruments and green
terrain.

111
00:08:53,930 --> 00:08:57,730
Display RGB values are coordinates for
represented color.

112
00:09:01,575 --> 00:09:05,235
Physical light need not contain exactly three
wavelengths;

113
00:09:09,101 --> 00:09:12,101
different spectra can appear alike.

114
00:09:15,986 --> 00:09:19,646
Pixels reflect materials, lighting, camera and
display transforms.

115
00:09:19,706 --> 00:09:27,411
A darker pixel alone does not prove a changed
material.

116
00:09:27,611 --> 00:09:36,397
Separate physical light and perceived color
before calculating energy quantities.

117
00:09:37,017 --> 00:09:40,337
Radiant energy is measured in joules.

118
00:09:40,477 --> 00:09:44,477
Four joules delivered over two seconds give an
average power of two watts.

119
00:09:44,897 --> 00:09:51,137
Radiant flux is total energy flow per time;
one watt equals one joule per second.

120
00:09:51,437 --> 00:09:54,117
If those two watts arrive uniformly across two
square metres,

121
00:09:54,117 --> 00:09:57,337
average irradiance is one watt per square
metre.

122
00:09:57,677 --> 00:10:02,397
Irradiance measures arrival per area and
exitance measures departure per area;

123
00:10:02,617 --> 00:10:05,937
total and density differ in units.

124
00:10:05,937 --> 00:10:08,577
Radiometry measures physical energy;

125
00:10:08,577 --> 00:10:13,077
photometry weights wavelengths by human visual
sensitivity.

126
00:10:13,357 --> 00:10:17,157
Distinguish watts from lumens and irradiance
from lux.

127
00:10:17,157 --> 00:10:22,357
Photometric quantities are not subjective
brightness or display gamma.

128
00:10:22,983 --> 00:10:29,243
Equal irradiance can arrive from many
directions or one narrow beam.

129
00:10:29,583 --> 00:10:32,283
Highlights require directional quantities.

130
00:10:32,523 --> 00:10:34,903
A solid angle corresponds to the projected
area of a

131
00:10:35,183 --> 00:10:40,263
region on a unit sphere around the observation
point.

132
00:10:40,443 --> 00:10:44,223
Its unit is the steradian: four pi for

133
00:10:44,223 --> 00:10:48,743
a sphere and two pi for a hemisphere.

134
00:10:49,043 --> 00:10:52,403
Radiance is flux per projected area per solid
angle,

135
00:10:52,403 --> 00:10:55,683
in watts per square metre per steradian.

136
00:10:56,043 --> 00:10:59,663
Projected area is measured perpendicular to
the ray

137
00:11:00,163 --> 00:11:03,503
direction and changes with surface
orientation.

138
00:11:04,043 --> 00:11:07,723
Irradiance and radiance cannot simply
substitute for one another;

139
00:11:08,343 --> 00:11:11,263
directional integration needs cosine and
solid-angle factors.

140
00:11:12,233 --> 00:11:17,614
Move with a handheld lamp beneath and beside
rock,

141
00:11:17,894 --> 00:11:20,774
observing illuminated patches and dark
neighboring faces.

142
00:11:22,875 --> 00:11:33,857
A curved rock has different local surface
orientations relative to incoming light.

143
00:11:34,057 --> 00:11:38,637
Distance, visibility, material and water
effects also matter.

144
00:11:40,697 --> 00:11:43,937
This recording is not a controlled angle-only
experiment.

145
00:11:46,258 --> 00:11:52,679
Our next diagram fixes illumination and
changes only a

146
00:11:52,679 --> 00:11:56,359
plate orientation to isolate projected-area
effects.

147
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:01,720
We compare explicitly defined zero and sixty
degrees

148
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:05,560
rather than inferring radiometric numbers from
the game.

149
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:09,780
For fixed directional illumination,

150
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:15,000
a plate normal aligned with the source
direction has angle zero and cosine one.

151
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:21,200
At sixty degrees, cosine is one half and the

152
00:12:21,540 --> 00:12:24,360
same actual area has half the projected area.

153
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:30,180
Its directional irradiance contribution halves
under these defined conditions,

154
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:34,000
independently of changing material response.

155
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:39,220
The dot product equals cosine only for

156
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:42,860
unit normal and unit direction vectors.

157
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:47,380
Our source-facing incident vector gives
positive front-side cosine;

158
00:12:47,740 --> 00:12:50,880
a light-travel vector requires the opposite
sign.

159
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:55,560
One-sided opaque direct lighting clamps
back-side contributions to zero;

160
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:58,380
two-sided transmission needs another model.

161
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:03,500
At one surface location, outgoing radiance

162
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:07,400
equals emitted radiance plus reflected
radiance.

163
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:10,340
Emissive surfaces have an emission term;

164
00:13:10,780 --> 00:13:14,100
ordinary nonemissive surfaces use zero for
that term.

165
00:13:14,380 --> 00:13:18,206
Integrate incident radiance times BRDF

166
00:13:18,206 --> 00:13:22,220
times cosine over the incident hemisphere.

167
00:13:22,320 --> 00:13:26,140
Hold the outgoing direction fixed while
summing

168
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:28,200
incident directions; the BRDF connects both.

169
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:31,280
BRDF inverse-steradian units cancel the

170
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:36,160
solid-angle integration, leaving radiance
units.

171
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:41,060
Location and wavelength specify the point and
spectral quantity;

172
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:45,060
camera and color transforms later produce
display pixels.

173
00:13:45,883 --> 00:13:49,143
Approach a bright base through green water,

174
00:13:49,383 --> 00:13:55,959
separating visible surfaces from arriving
light.

175
00:13:58,474 --> 00:14:04,094
A light emitting and a wall receiving and
reflecting light play different roles.

176
00:14:06,850 --> 00:14:10,390
Next the player installs a ceiling light;

177
00:14:10,806 --> 00:14:14,066
observe the construction preview becoming an
installed fixture.

178
00:14:14,542 --> 00:14:19,342
Broadly brighter walls and ceiling after
placement connect those roles,

179
00:14:19,622 --> 00:14:22,933
rather than proving that every wall became
emissive.

180
00:14:22,933 --> 00:14:26,973
We do not identify the game indirect-lighting
implementation.

181
00:14:28,287 --> 00:14:31,427
The diagram explains light transport
relationships and cost.

182
00:14:32,167 --> 00:14:35,107
Direct lighting arrives from a light;

183
00:14:35,387 --> 00:14:38,887
indirect lighting arrives after another
surface interaction.

184
00:14:39,127 --> 00:14:41,587
One surface outgoing radiance becomes another

185
00:14:41,667 --> 00:14:44,747
surface incident radiance along an unoccluded
path.

186
00:14:45,087 --> 00:14:48,987
Thus evaluating one point can require
evaluating

187
00:14:49,207 --> 00:14:52,187
other points: the equation couples the scene.

188
00:14:52,447 --> 00:14:59,767
More bounces increase work; visibility,
shadows, reflection and paths all matter.

189
00:14:59,767 --> 00:15:04,207
Real-time budgets motivate precomputation,

190
00:15:04,207 --> 00:15:08,567
limited paths, screen-space estimates and
hybrid methods.

191
00:15:08,907 --> 00:15:12,907
Ray tracing also has finite samples, path
limits and model error.

192
00:15:13,347 --> 00:15:16,287
Examine computed and omitted effects rather
than technique names.

193
00:15:17,183 --> 00:15:20,963
Walk among rocks and trees, comparing lit
ground

194
00:15:23,851 --> 00:15:28,111
with shadow while camera motion changes the
view.

195
00:15:30,959 --> 00:15:34,139
Shadow can mean one light direction is
blocked,

196
00:15:37,047 --> 00:15:40,267
without making all incident directions zero.

197
00:15:43,154 --> 00:15:45,634
Surrounding surfaces and sky can contribute;

198
00:15:45,874 --> 00:15:51,582
one directional value is not the whole
illumination.

199
00:15:51,822 --> 00:15:54,982
Focus on summing directional contributions
without

200
00:15:55,402 --> 00:15:58,282
identifying this game shadow or
indirect-lighting method.

201
00:15:58,282 --> 00:16:06,450
Our final example explains why doubling sample
count should not double expected brightness.

202
00:16:07,150 --> 00:16:10,850
Numerical integration samples directions

203
00:16:11,210 --> 00:16:14,750
with weights for their represented solid
angle.

204
00:16:15,110 --> 00:16:19,750
Simply adding more unweighted samples makes
brightness

205
00:16:20,030 --> 00:16:22,150
scale with count rather than estimating the
same integral.

206
00:16:22,470 --> 00:16:26,750
Uniform hemisphere sampling has density one
over two pi.

207
00:16:26,910 --> 00:16:31,410
Divide each contribution by its density and
average over samples.

208
00:16:31,630 --> 00:16:37,590
Doubling normalized samples preserves expected
brightness while potentially changing noise.

209
00:16:37,930 --> 00:16:41,090
Ideal point lights require separate total-flux
and sampling

210
00:16:41,610 --> 00:16:46,510
treatment beyond an ordinary finite-area
function.

211
00:16:46,850 --> 00:16:50,610
Multiply and sum light in linear color values,

212
00:16:50,890 --> 00:16:54,890
not directly in gamma-encoded display values.

213
00:16:55,517 --> 00:16:59,717
Walk beside the perforated structure,

214
00:16:59,717 --> 00:17:09,025
then observe differently colored characters
overlapping. Identify the foreground surface.

215
00:17:15,133 --> 00:17:20,697
Compare bright and dark faces separately

216
00:17:20,937 --> 00:17:25,117
from their front-to-back ordering.

217
00:17:25,317 --> 00:17:32,741
Neither surface color nor orientation alone
determines

218
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:41,049
pixels: connect arriving light with
directional reflection.

219
00:17:41,049 --> 00:17:44,589
Review three steps: select the surface, keep
quantities consistent,

220
00:17:44,589 --> 00:17:50,113
then integrate directional reflection.

221
00:17:50,333 --> 00:17:55,257
Our last defined diffuse example calculates
outgoing

222
00:17:55,517 --> 00:17:59,497
radiance from reflectance and received
irradiance.

223
00:18:00,117 --> 00:18:03,877
First: with smaller-is-nearer toy depths red
eight and blue three,

224
00:18:03,877 --> 00:18:08,757
either processing order leaves blue.

225
00:18:09,117 --> 00:18:13,437
Next define a nonemissive ideal Lambertian
surface with

226
00:18:13,657 --> 00:18:18,237
reflectance zero point six and irradiance ten
watts per square metre.

227
00:18:18,597 --> 00:18:21,057
Its BRDF is reflectance divided by pi;
outgoing radiance is zero

228
00:18:21,217 --> 00:18:26,297
point six times ten divided by pi,
approximately one point nine one.

229
00:18:26,517 --> 00:18:29,697
Radiance units are watts per square metre per
steradian.

230
00:18:29,697 --> 00:18:34,657
Exitance is six watts per square metre, a
different quantity.

231
00:18:35,057 --> 00:18:38,137
These are explicitly defined reflectance and
illumination conditions,

232
00:18:38,317 --> 00:18:42,057
not values measured from game display pixels.

233
00:18:42,337 --> 00:18:46,677
Select a surface, distinguish quantities,
connect light with material and view.

234
00:18:47,037 --> 00:18:51,697
Next we connect camera and projection
coordinates to map3D points to the screen.
